course: dictionary
lesson: 11880
title: Braised: a word from the kitchen
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from cooking, and the food around them gives you clues to their meaning. «Braised» is one of these words. When you see «braised beef» on a menu, the word tells you how the beef was cooked, not its colour or its size.
explain: To braise food, you first brown it in hot fat so the outside turns golden. Then you cook it slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid. «She braised the carrots» uses braised as the past tense of braise: it tells you what she did in the past.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. «The slow-braised lamb was soft enough to cut with a spoon.» Slow and soft are the clues. Check your meaning by swapping it in: «cooked slowly in a covered pot» makes sense in place of braised, so you know you are right.
fact: Braise means to cook food slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid, after browning it in hot fat.
fact: «Braised» is the past tense of «braise»: «She braised the beef.»
fact: «Braised» can also describe food on a menu, as in «braised red cabbage» or «braised lamb shanks».
fact: Slow braising makes tough meat soft and tender.
fact: You may see the phrases «slow-braised» and «braised in», as in «braised in stock».
exercise: «The cook braised the lamb in a covered pot.» What does braised mean here? | Painted with bright colours | *Cooked slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid | Chopped into tiny pieces
exercise: Ruby reads «braised red cabbage» on a menu. What does braised tell her? | How many people will eat it | *How the cabbage was cooked | How big the cabbage is | The colour of the cabbage
exercise: Find and copy the past tense of «braise» in this sentence: «Dad braises the carrots today, and yesterday he braised the peas.» | carrots | braises | *braised | yesterday
test: «The slow-braised beef was soft and tender.» What does slow-braised tell you? | Grilled very fast over a flame | Eaten raw straight from the farm | *Cooked slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid | Frozen quickly in a freezer
test: Which sentence uses braised as the past tense of braise? | *Yesterday the chef braised the onions. | The chef is braising the onions now. | The chef braises the onions every day. | The onions braise well.
exam: Mia thinks «braised» means bruised in «braised lamb shanks». What is the correct meaning? | Served cold on a plate | Bruised, with dark marks on the skin | Cut into thin slices | *Cooked slowly in a covered pot with a little liquid, not bruised
